# ClubMatch — Design Document

Course: CS5610 Web Development — Northeastern University
Authors: Kanad Motiwale and Aarya Patil
Date: February 2026

---

## Project Description

Every semester at Northeastern, students go through the same cycle. They show up to the involvement fair, grab a bunch of flyers, follow a few Instagram pages, and still have no real idea what joining a club actually looks like day to day. How many hours does it really take? Is it worth it for someone with a packed schedule? There is no good answer to these questions anywhere, and that is the problem we wanted to solve.

ClubMatch is a web platform where students can browse clubs, read honest participation logs from people who have actually been in those clubs, and set up a personal profile to represent themselves in the community. The idea is simple — put all the information in one place and let students make better decisions.

The app has four independent parts. The Club Directory, owned by Kanad, is a browsable and filterable list of student organizations with key information like category and weekly time commitment. The Membership Logs, also owned by Kanad, are structured entries from real members sharing how many hours they actually spent and what they got out of it. The Student Profiles section, owned by Aarya, lets students create personal profiles listing their major, year, interests, and clubs they have joined. The Authentication system, also owned by Aarya, lets students register and log in to a personal account with a hashed password, giving them a more connected experience across the platform.

All collections run on the same Express backend and MongoDB database but are completely independent with no foreign keys or references between them.

### Work Distribution

Kanad Motiwale handled the full CRUD stack for the clubs collection and the membership logs collection, including an endpoint that calculates average weekly hours per club across all submitted logs.

Aarya Patil handled the full CRUD stack for the users collection, covering profile creation, search, editing, and deletion. Aarya also built the full authentication system including account registration, login, and bcrypt password hashing.

### Technology Stack

The backend is Node.js and Express using ES Modules only. The database is MongoDB Atlas using the native driver with no Mongoose. Passwords are hashed using bcrypt before being stored. The frontend is plain HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript with client side rendering and no frameworks. Code quality tools include ESLint and Prettier. The app is deployed on Render.com.

### MongoDB Collections

The clubs collection belongs to Kanad and stores the club name, category, description, weekly time commitment, and timestamps.

The membership logs collection also belongs to Kanad and stores the club name, member name, weekly hours, benefits, challenges, and timestamps.

The users collection belongs to Aarya and stores the student name, email, hashed password, major, year, bio, interests as an array, joined clubs as an array, and a creation timestamp.

No foreign keys or references exist between any of the collections. Each collection stands completely on its own.

