## User Personas

### Persona 1 — Alex, First-Year Student

Alex is 18 years old, undeclared in his major, and in his first week at Northeastern. He showed up to the involvement fair and was honestly overwhelmed. There were dozens of tables, everyone was pitching their club, and he had maybe 5 hours a week to spare. He followed a few Instagram accounts but never really got useful information back. He just wants somewhere to sit down, look through his options without the noise, and figure out what actually makes sense for him.

His goals are to build a profile that reflects who he is and what he is looking for, to filter clubs by how much time they actually take, and to read what other students genuinely thought of a club before committing. His frustrations are that every club looks great on a flyer with no way to tell what it is really like, that he has to dig through multiple Instagram pages and GroupMe chats just to find basic information, and that no one tells him upfront how many hours per week something actually takes.

### Persona 2 — Priya, Junior Computer Science Student

Priya is 21 years old and has been around long enough to know how this works. She has been in a few clubs, dropped a couple because they took way more time than advertised, and is now pretty selective. She wants to update her profile each semester and she likes having data to back up her decisions. If the average member logs 6 hours a week, she knows to think twice.

Her goals are to keep her profile current without it being a hassle, to quickly filter clubs by time commitment before even reading the description, and to use membership log data to actually compare clubs rather than just their marketing. Her frustrations are that university portals are clunky and do not show anything useful about club workload, that she keeps finding outdated club pages with no recent activity, and that there is no way to share what she learned from being in a club so others do not make the same mistakes.

### Persona 3 — Aisha, Graduate Student and Club Administrator

Aisha is 23 years old and manages the South Asian Cultural Club. Her biggest headache is keeping the club's information up to date. Right now she has to email someone in student affairs and wait a few days for even a small change to go through. By then interested students have already moved on. She wants control over her own listing.

Her goals are to add the club to the platform herself and keep it updated without waiting on anyone, to make sure the time commitment listed is honest so they attract the right members, and to remove old or wrong information quickly when things change. Her frustrations are that the current process for updating club information is painfully slow, that she has no visibility into whether students are even finding the club's page, and that members have no structured way to share their experience so recruitment relies entirely on word of mouth.

